        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "You can register interceptors to apply to incoming requests, as the following example shows:"
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "include-code::./WebConfiguration[tag=snippet,indent=0]"
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "admonition",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "Interceptors are not ideally suited as a security layer due to the potential for a mismatch with annotated controller path matching. Generally, we recommend using Spring Security, or alternatively a similar approach integrated with the Servlet filter chain, and applied as early as possible."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null,
        "type": "warning"
      },
      {
        "context": "admonition",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "The XML config declares interceptors as "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "MappedInterceptor"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " beans, and those are in turn detected by any "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "HandlerMapping"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " bean, including those from other frameworks. By contrast, the Java config passes interceptors only to the "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "HandlerMapping"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " beans it manages. To re-use the same interceptors across Spring MVC and other framework "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "HandlerMapping"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " beans with the MVC Java config, either declare "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "MappedInterceptor"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " beans (and don’t manually add them in the Java config), or configure the same interceptors in both the Java config and in other "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "HandlerMapping"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " beans."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null,
        "type": "note"
      }
    ],
    "outline": null,
